Tire carbon black is a finely divided carbon material used primarily as a reinforcing agent in tire rubber compounds. It improves tensile strength, abrasion resistance, durability, resistance to weathering, and heat dissipation. These properties make it an essential material for producing tires capable of meeting modern performance and safety requirements.

The market is closely connected to global vehicle production, tire replacement cycles, commercial transportation, and the development of high-performance tires. Demand is also being shaped by requirements for lower rolling resistance, improved wet grip, and longer tread life. At the same time, fluctuations in petroleum-derived feedstock prices and stricter environmental regulations are encouraging manufacturers to improve production efficiency and explore circular feedstocks.

